    <div class="jr-prose">

      <h2 id="why">Is collagen vegetarian?</h2>

      <p>
        No. Collagen is the main structural protein in animal skin, bone, tendon and cartilage, and no
        plant makes it. Every collagen supplement that actually contains collagen is extracted from
        animals: fish skin and scales, cow hide and bone, pig skin, or chicken cartilage.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is not a technicality. Plants hold themselves up with cellulose and other fibres, not with
        protein fibres like collagen, so there is nothing to extract. When a label says vegetarian and
        collagen in the same breath, one of those two words is doing more work than it should.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-table-scroll">
        <table class="jr-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Product type</th><th scope="col">Contains collagen?</th><th scope="col">Vegetarian?</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Marine collagen (fish)</th><td>Yes</td><td>No. Non-veg, and avoid with fish allergy</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Bovine collagen (cow)</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Porcine collagen (pig)</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Chicken type II collagen</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">"Plant-based collagen" or "collagen builder"</th><td>No. Vitamins, minerals and amino acids</td><td>Usually yes</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Fermentation-made collagen-like protein</th><td>A lab-made protein modelled on collagen</td><td>Animal-free, but human evidence is still missing</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="plant">What is in plant-based collagen?</h2>

      <p>
        Usually no collagen at all. Most products sold as vegan or plant-based collagen in India are
        blends of the nutrients your body uses to make its own collagen: vitamin C, zinc, copper,
        silica, and amino acids such as glycine, proline and lysine, often with amla, bamboo extract or
        herbal additions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some of that logic is sound. Vitamin C is required for the step that stabilises newly made
        collagen, and genuine vitamin C deficiency does impair it. But for most people eating a
        reasonable diet, these co-factors are not the bottleneck, and no trial we could find shows a
        plant-based collagen builder improving skin the way the hydrolysed collagen trials report. They
        are a different product making a similar promise.
      </p>

      <p>
        The one genuinely new category is lab-made collagen. In 2021 a US biodesign company launched a
        collagen-like protein made by microbial fermentation, matching the core of a rare type of
        collagen and containing no animal ingredients. It is a real scientific achievement. It is also
        early: we could not find independent, published human results for it, so it cannot yet be
        compared with marine or bovine collagen on evidence.
      </p>

      <h2 id="label">How to check a collagen label in India</h2>

      <p>
        Look at the symbol on the front of the pack before you read a single claim. Under FSSAI
        labelling regulations, food containing any part of an animal, including fish and other marine
        animals, is non-vegetarian and must carry the brown symbol: a brown filled triangle inside a
        square with a brown outline. Vegetarian food carries the green filled circle.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>The rule has no minimum.</strong> Since 2022, if any constituent of a compound ingredient is of animal origin, the pack must carry the non-veg logo regardless of the percentage.</li>
        <li><strong>It applies to supplements.</strong> FSSAI has said health supplements with animal-origin ingredients that carry the green dot are misbranded.</li>
        <li><strong>It cannot be dropped for small packs.</strong> The veg or non-veg logo must appear on the front of the pack.</li>
        <li><strong>If a collagen product shows a green dot,</strong> it either contains no collagen, or it is mislabelled. Read the ingredient list to find out which.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="ours">Is marine collagen veg or non-veg?</h2>

      <p>
        Non-veg. Marine collagen is made from fish, usually the skin and scales, and fish count as
        non-vegetarian under Indian labelling rules. Many people who avoid meat assume fish collagen is
        somehow different. It is not, and if you are vegetarian for religious, ethical or family
        reasons, you should not take it.
      </p>

      <p>
        That includes ours. Collabloom contains 8,000mg and Skelecoll 10,000mg of Japanese hydrolysed
        marine collagen per shot, so both are non-vegetarian, not suitable for Jains, and not suitable
        for anyone allergic to fish. Marine collagen avoids the beef question that rules bovine collagen
        out for many Indian buyers, a point our article on
        <a href="/pages/marine-vs-bovine-collagen">marine vs bovine collagen</a> covers in detail.
        That makes it acceptable to more people. It does not make it vegetarian.
      </p>

