      <h2 id="vegetarians">What can vegetarians do instead?</h2>

      <p>
        Protect and support the collagen you already make. These steps have better evidence than any
        plant-based collagen blend, and none of them involve an animal product.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Wear sunscreen every day and reapply it.</strong> Sun exposure is the biggest avoidable driver of collagen breakdown in skin, which our guide to <a href="/pages/boost-collagen-naturally">boosting collagen naturally</a> explains.</li>
        <li><strong>Eat enough total protein.</strong> Collagen is built from amino acids that come from all dietary protein. Dal, paneer, curd, soy, tofu, chana and rajma all count. Reviews note plant proteins are generally less anabolic than animal protein because of lower leucine and digestibility, so combining sources and eating adequate portions matters.</li>
        <li><strong>Get vitamin C from food.</strong> Amla, guava, citrus, capsicum and leafy greens cover the requirement for most people.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not smoke,</strong> and keep blood sugar under control, both of which accelerate collagen damage.</li>
        <li><strong>Use topical retinoids</strong> under a dermatologist's guidance, which have far stronger evidence for collagen in skin than any supplement, plant or animal.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        If you eat fish and are comfortable with a fish-derived product, a hydrolysed marine collagen is a
        reasonable option to try, and our article on <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">collagen for
        skin</a> sets out what it can and cannot do. If you do not, nothing in this article should push
        you to start.
      </p>

