      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Is collagen vegetarian?</summary>
          <p>No. Collagen is an animal protein, so every supplement that genuinely contains collagen is made from fish, cows, pigs or chicken. Products labelled plant-based collagen usually contain no collagen, only nutrients that support the body's own production.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is there a vegetarian collagen in India?</summary>
          <p>Not a real one. Vegetarian products sold as collagen are blends of vitamin C, zinc, copper and amino acids. Lab-made, animal-free collagen-like proteins exist but have no published human evidence yet.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is marine collagen veg or non-veg?</summary>
          <p>Non-veg. Marine collagen is made from fish, and FSSAI rules require products containing any marine animal ingredient to carry the brown non-vegetarian symbol.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is Collabloom vegetarian?</summary>
          <p>No. Collabloom contains 8,000mg of hydrolysed marine collagen from fish, so it is non-vegetarian, not suitable for Jains, and not suitable for anyone with a fish allergy. The same applies to Skelecoll.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do plant-based collagen boosters work?</summary>
          <p>They supply co-factors such as vitamin C, zinc and copper that the body uses to make collagen. These only make a difference if you are actually short of them. We could not find trials showing them improving skin the way hydrolysed collagen trials report.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How can vegetarians increase collagen naturally?</summary>
          <p>Daily sunscreen, enough total protein from dal, paneer, curd and soy, vitamin C from amla and fruit, not smoking, and topical retinoids under a dermatologist's guidance. These protect and support the collagen you already make.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can Jains take marine collagen?</summary>
          <p>No. Marine collagen comes from fish and is non-vegetarian, so it is not compatible with a Jain diet. Look for the brown non-veg symbol on the front of any collagen pack.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

